  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Tennessee federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date total $158M. The state recorded 432 awards with an average award size of $365K. Obligations fell 98.7% year over year. Fiscal-year comparisons show prior-year totals of $11.4B in FY22, $11.2B in FY23, $10.8B in FY24, $11B in FY25, and $12.3B in FY26, placing the current fiscal-year-to-date total well below recent full-year results. The Department of Veterans Affairs is the largest buyer in FY2027 to date with $144M. The Department of Justice follows with $14.8M, and the General Services Administration follows with $1.3M. VALIANT CONSTRUCTION LLC is the top vendor with $22.3M, followed by VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY MEDICAL CENTER with $15.4M and FEDEX SUPPLY CHAIN DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM, INC. with $9M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction leads industry spending with $23.8M, and Colleges, Universities, and Professional Schools follow with $21.9M. The current-year totals are partial. Contracting activity is concentrated in a few large buyers, a handful of top vendors, and specific industries, which together account for most FY2027 to date obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is FY2027 federal contracting in Tennessee?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $144M, concentrating most FY2027 to date dollars in one agency. The top three agencies together account for a large share of the $158M total, with the Department of Justice at $14.8M and the General Services Administration at $1.3M. VALIANT CONSTRUCTION LLC and a few other vendors received the largest shares of those obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What industries and vendors dominate FY2027 spending in Tennessee?",
        "body": "Commercial and Institutional Building Construction received $23.8M, the largest industry share, followed by Colleges, Universities, and Professional Schools at $21.9M. VALIANT CONSTRUCTION LLC received $22.3M and VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY MEDICAL CENTER received $15.4M, placing construction and higher-education-related vendors among the biggest recipients. Several healthcare and logistics firms also appear in the top vendor list."
      }
    ]
  },
